<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10018"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iq<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iq<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 01, 2005 - 5:35 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Check out this post from another board from April of 2004. I bet Rader freaked if he saw this: <BR> <BR>Follow this trail with me, please: <BR> <BR>The murders are all near city parks. City parks are open areas. Open areas attract &#34;non-pet&#34; people and people with their pets, usually dogs. <BR> <BR>So, if BTK was an animal control officer, or worked with animal control officers, he would: <BR> <BR>1. Have access to police and police activity from working with them on animal involved cases. Might be allowed to enforce certain laws pertaining to animal control. <BR> <BR>2. Might have had a badge back then. <BR> <BR>3. Could go on private property using a &#34;complaint&#34; as an excuse, as in searching for loose dog at nearly any hour. <BR> <BR>4. Could hang around city parks without arousing too much suspicion. <BR> <BR>5. Wouldn&#39;t be too intimidated by homeowners pets. <BR> <BR>6. If he was an animal torturer in his youth he&#39;d be around all sorts of unwanted animals most of the day to act out fantasies. <BR> <BR>7. Could possibly enter residences with out force as in flashing the badge and wanting to discuss a pet issue. <BR> <BR>8. Knife, plastic bags and rope to contain/control/manage animals wouldn&#39;t be unusual for him to possess. <BR> <BR>9. Would have experience in &#34;descriptive skills&#34; used for incident reports. Could have camera skills for same reason. <BR> <BR>I&#39;ve probably left off some additional similarities but the dog walking/city parks discussions caused me to wonder who is involved with those two situations that might be suspect and that led to the animal control theory. <BR> <BR> <BR>But for every post like this there were 100 others that were no where close.. <BR> <BR>iq <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10018-->
<!--Post: 10020--><!--Time: 1109727850-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10020"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 01, 2005 - 5:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->He wasn&#39;t in code enforcement/animal control at the time of the eight previously known murders that were linked to him. He was a security alarm installer for ADT at those times and a WSU student.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10020-->
