<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10584"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Essevi<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Essevi<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 05, 2005 - 6:50 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Jack5150- that was reported the day after Rader&#39;s arrest. We now know that it was incorrect. Below is a recent article explaining how the daughters DNA was aquired.  <BR>   <BR>Daughter DNA tissue tested <BR> <BR>Delaina Renfro <BR>KWCH 12 Eyewitness News <BR>Thursday, March 3, 2005  <BR>  <BR>The daughter of BTK suspect, Dennis Rader, may have never known she helped the investigation of her father. According to a Wichita Eagle source, investigators got DNA from a tissue sample. <BR> <BR>They got that sample from the daughter&#39;s medical records in Kansas. Police can use samples from your medical tests too and they don&#39;t have to tell you. <BR> <BR>“In the criminal court, in a case involving say BTK, it doesn&#39;t matter who the patient is- the patient&#39;s family member, loved one, offspring, sister, brother- there are no rights.” Said medical malpractice attorney, Mark Hutton. <BR> <BR>In criminal cases, your medical records are free game and you may never know about it. <BR> <BR>“The law enforcement authority has the right to investigate and not give the patient, the suspect the person of interest any heads up as to why they are investigating or what they are investigating.” Hutton explained. <BR> <BR>Hutton says your rights to protect your own privacy are different in a civil court case. <BR> <BR>“HIPAA &#40;Act&#41; is the federal legislation that guarantees privacy in a civil court case involving civil parties but in the criminal court involving a crime or a suspect your HIPAA rights are excluded. You don&#39;t have any HIPAA rights.” <BR> <BR>Investigators also took DNA a test from Rader&#39;s daughter in Michigan where she now lives. But the family&#39;s pastor has said she would have given her DNA only to exclude her father as a suspect, not to incriminate him. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kwch.com/servlet/Satellite?pagename=KWCH%2FMGArticle%2FWCH_BasicArticle&amp;c=MGArticle&amp;cid=1031781373777&amp;path=" target=_top>http://www.kwch.com/servlet/Satellite?pagename=KWC H%2FMGArticle%2FWCH_BasicArticle&amp;c=MGArticle&amp;cid=1 031781373777&amp;path=</a>!news!crime <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
