<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11084"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iq<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iq<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 09, 2005 - 5:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B- I think you missed Lizzie&#39;s question. I believe she was inferring that Rader may have been the one that ticketed Valadez for the code violations that LE fell back on after they realized they had made a big...uh...&#34;boo boo&#34;? <BR> <BR>Anyway, from what I can gather, Rader only enforced code violations etc. inside Park City limits. Mr. V lived in the city of Wichita. I don&#39;t think he could have been the one. However, I think that the recent article in the Eagle detailing the fact that Mr. V&#39;s attorneys are having to file a motion to obtain the information that led WPD to him as a POI shows that something very interesting transpired. <BR> <BR>I can&#39;t even phathom what that may have been though. I have run through scenarios in my head and can&#39;t come up with any way Raders job as a compliance officer led them to Valadez, just a strange coincidence. I am curious to see if they release that information once the attorneys and Valadez get ahold of it. <BR> <BR>iq<!--/Text--></td></tr>
